<http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/topic/external-property-by-wittgenstein> a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "An object has internal and external properties: the internal properties are its logical form, what kind of object it is and how it can combine with other objects in states of affairs. The external properties are what is true of it, what states of affairs it does occur in." ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:topics ;
    schema:name "External Property" .

<http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/topic/formal-concept-by-wittgenstein> a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "A concept proper, for example 'x is a stone', is something we can meaningfully talk about simply because it erfers to a state of affairs which can be depicted by our language. This notion stands in opposition to the one of formal concept. This latter defines attempts to define the formal properties of an object, of a state of affairs or a fact. As such, they are nonsensical as they try to depict the same form of representation, i.e. the logical form. Examples of formal concepts can be 'two is a number' or 'purple is a color'." ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:topics ;
    schema:name "Formal Concept" .

<http://wittgensteiniana.michelepasin.org/id/topic/prop-ethics-concept-g614703> a schema:DefinedTerm ;
    schema:description "According to Wittgenstein, the propositions of aesthetics, as well as the propositions of ethics, cannot be put into words because they make judgements of value. Therefore, they are clearly nonsensical" ;
    schema:inDefinedTermSet id:topics ;
    schema:name "Propositions of Ethics" .

